# dotfiles-MacBook/ssh/hosts.conf.example → seeded to ~/.ssh/config.d/10-hosts.conf
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A STARTER file, not a live config. bootstrap.sh copies it (Core's blib_seed) to
# ~/.ssh/config.d/10-hosts.conf only when that file is ABSENT, and never touches it
# again — so the copy is yours to edit and no bootstrap can clobber it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# ~/.ssh/config is a SYMLINK into core/ssh/config, and core/ is a vendored git
# subtree of dotfiles-core. Host stanzas written there are reverted by the next
# Core sync, blocked by the pre-commit guard, and rejected by the core-integrity
# CI job — they can never be saved. One was lost exactly that way. This file, and
# the ~/.ssh/config.d/ drop-in it seeds, is where host stanzas actually belong.
#
# WHY THE COPY IS NOT TRACKED
# ---------------------------
# Real host stanzas name internal addresses, ports and usernames, and this repo is
# PUBLIC. So the SEED is tracked (allowlisted in .gitignore beside ssh/os.conf) and
# carries placeholders only; the seeded copy in $HOME is untracked and carries the
# real thing. Never paste a real host into this file.
#
# The tracked/untracked split, end to end:
#
#   ssh/hosts.conf.example  (tracked, placeholders)  --seed-->  ~/.ssh/config.d/10-hosts.conf
#   ssh/os.conf             (tracked, public-safe)   --link-->  ~/.ssh/config.d/50-os.conf
#   core/ssh/config         (vendored, read-only)    --link-->  ~/.ssh/config
#
# PRECEDENCE
# ----------
# core/ssh/config Includes ~/.ssh/config.d/*.conf as its FIRST directive, and ssh is
# first-obtained-value-wins, so drop-ins beat Core's `Host *` defaults. The numeric
# prefixes are the tie-break between drop-ins: 10- is reached before 50-.
#
# IdentityFile is the one keyword that ACCUMULATES instead of first-winning — a key
# named here is OFFERED FIRST but does not displace Core's ~/.ssh/id_ed25519. A host
# that must offer exactly one key needs `IdentitiesOnly yes` alongside it.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Give a host a short alias by listing both names on the Host line: the alias for
# typing, the real address so the long form keeps working too.
#
#   Host myserver 192.0.2.10
#     HostName 192.0.2.10
#     Port 2222
#     User me
#
# A host behind a bastion:
#
#   Host internal
#     HostName internal.example.invalid
#     User me
#     ProxyJump bastion.example.invalid
#
# A host with its own key, offered first and exclusively:
#
#   Host deploy
#     HostName deploy.example.invalid
#     User deploy
#     IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_deploy
#     IdentitiesOnly yes

# Verify any stanza you add without connecting:
#   ssh -G myserver | grep -E '^(hostname|port|user) '
